# Copy to deploy/.env (gitignored) and fill in.

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Access keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Named, scoped, hashed. Mint each one with:
#   cd server-portable && bun keygen.ts --name laptop  --scope write
#   cd server-portable && bun keygen.ts --name chatgpt --scope read
#
# Store the HASHES here, never the keys — the server only ever compares digests,
# so this value is not a usable credential on its own. Comma- or newline-separated.
#
# Give read scope to anything that only searches, and to any client that needs the
# key in a URL: capture_thought is not registered for a read key, so it cannot
# write even if the URL leaks into logs or browser history.
MCP_ACCESS_KEYS=

# Each key name here becomes an agent in ob1_agents on first use, with a stable
# id that survives both rotating the key (keep the name, change the digest) and
# renaming it (keep the digest, change the name). Every write is attributed to
# that id in thought_audit.canonical_agent_id.
#
# To revoke a key WITHOUT editing this file and restarting, take its digest from
# above and run against the database:
#
#   SELECT revoke_agent_key('<digest>', 'why');
#
# It takes effect within OB1_AGENT_CACHE_TTL_MS and the agent's history stays
# intact and queryable. Revocation here can only ever be more restrictive than
# this file, never less.
# OB1_AGENT_CACHE_TTL_MS=60000

# ── Model provider ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two calls happen on every capture: an embedding (load-bearing — without it a
# thought is invisible to search) and a metadata extraction (enrichment — topics,
# people, type; a failure here degrades gracefully).
#
# Pick ONE of the two blocks below.

# ── Option A: OpenRouter (default) ───────────────────────────────────────────
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OB1_EMBEDDING_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM=1536   # hosted; unmeasured, see evals/README.md
# OB1_METADATA_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini

# ── Option B: fully local, nothing leaves the host ───────────────────────────
# Start with:  podman compose -f deploy/compose.yaml --profile local-models up --build
# Leave OPENROUTER_API_KEY empty; a loopback provider needs no credential.
#
# Chosen by measurement, not size — see evals/README.md.
# OB1_LLM_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
# The default. Best measured on real data (0.933 MRR vs embeddinggemma's 0.914)
# and the only local model that embeds a long capture whole. 2560 native, so it
# relies on Matryoshka truncation to fit pgvector's 2000-dimension index limit —
# enabled automatically for known-MRL models, no flag needed.
# OB1_EMBEDDING_MODEL=qwen3-embedding:4b
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM=1024
#
# Cheaper alternatives, both ~620 MB against 2.5 GB and ~3x faster:
#   qwen3-embedding:0.6b @ 1024   0.918 MRR
#   embeddinggemma       @ 768    0.916 MRR
# OB1_METADATA_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b

# ── The embedding width is permanent ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Ask the provider for a NARROWER vector than the model natively emits, via the
# OpenAI `dimensions` parameter. Off unless set to on/1/true/yes.
#
# This is what makes a model wider than pgvector's 2000-dimension HNSW ceiling
# usable: qwen3-embedding:4b emits 2560 and cannot be indexed, but truncated to
# 1024 it scored the best retrieval result measured on real data. Set
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM to the width you want and turn this on.
#
# Only safe for models trained for Matryoshka truncation (embeddinggemma,
# qwen3-embedding, openai/text-embedding-3-*). Providers apply the parameter to
# ANY model and return a shortened vector with no warning — on a model not trained
# for it, retrieval quietly gets worse. Measured: roughly twice the MRR loss.
# See evals/README.md.
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=on

# Trigram index on thoughts.content. This is what makes search_thoughts_keyword
# fast; without it that tool still returns the right rows, by sequential scan.
#
# ON by default since migration 012 gave the index a caller. It was off before
# that, for the narrow reason that no core query could reach it.
#
# Measured rather than assumed (db/bench-trgm.ts, db/bench-keyword.ts). Below
# ~10,000 rows the planner declines it and it changes nothing; above that a
# 5-row match improves ~350x at 10k and ~1350x at 100k, but a word in 10% of
# rows gets only 8-9x, and a common word or a pattern under three characters
# gets nothing at any size. What it always costs is ~70-95 microseconds on every
# capture and roughly the size of the table again in storage.
#
# So set this to `off` if your brain is small and staying small: you would be
# paying the write cost for a read benefit the planner will not take. Everything
# keeps working.
#
# Read only when migration 011 first applies. Change it later and preflight will
# tell you the setting and the database disagree, with the statement to run —
# which is the state every deployment that applied 011 before 012 is now in.
# OB1_TRGM_INDEX=off

# Chunking. A capture longer than the provider's per-request batch (2048 tokens on
# Ollama by default) is embedded only in part, so its tail becomes unsearchable.
# Above these many estimated tokens the server splits the capture into overlapping
# windows and embeds each, keeping the whole thing findable. Short thoughts — nearly
# all of them — are untouched and write no extra rows.
# OB1_CHUNK_TOKENS=1200
# OB1_CHUNK_OVERLAP=150

# Contextual retrieval: generate a short blurb situating each window in its parent
# document and prepend it before embedding, so a window reading "we settled on
# thirty minutes" carries which system it concerns.
#
# OFF, and measured off rather than assumed off. On 37 queries that name a
# document's subject and ask for a detail living in one window, against the
# default `qwen3-embedding:4b`, it is WORSE than the bare windows this server used
# to store — 0.826 MRR against 0.904, helping one query and hurting eight — and
# further behind what it stores now, which is 0.935. The cause is
# measured too: the blurb moves a window away from its own query, because a
# fixed-size vector has less room for the sentence that actually answers.
#
# Turn it on only after measuring on YOUR corpus with `evals/eval-contextual.ts`.
# The sign depends on the model: the same harness on `embeddinggemma` — 768
# dimensions and a real 2048-token ceiling — reports +0.041 instead. A weaker
# window vector has more to gain from the extra subject signal.
#
# Costs one LLM call per chunk at capture (~1.6s at qwen2.5:7b locally), on the
# few captures long enough to chunk. Needs migration 013; preflight refuses to
# start if this is on without it, because the blurbs would be embedded and then
# silently discarded.
# OB1_CHUNK_CONTEXT=on

# The model and OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM must agree, and CANNOT change once you have
# captured anything without re-embedding every row. Choose now, while empty.
#   openai/text-embedding-3-small  1536
#   embeddinggemma                  768   best measured local option
#   bge-m3                         1024   ties it; multilingual
#   nomic-embed-text                768   measurably worse — see evals/
# text-embedding-3-large returns 3072, which exceeds pgvector's HNSW limit of
# 2000 — the column works but no index can be built, so every search is a scan.
# migrate.ts refuses a mismatched pair, and preflight refuses a schema that
# disagrees with the running config.

# Only needed if your provider is not OpenRouter and not local.
# OB1_LLM_API_KEY=

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPEN_BRAIN_CITATION_BASE_URL=https://your-dashboard.example/thoughts
# OB1_PG_POOL=10
# SERVER_PORT=8000
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
